WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.937
A Spark application has a driver and executors.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:08.082
The driver coordinates the application and builds the plan.

00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:11.897
Executors run tasks on data partitions.

00:00:11.897 --> 00:00:17.072
A beginner-friendly way to think about this is that the driver organizes the work and the executors perform

00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:26.072
the work. We should avoid collecting huge data back to the driver because that can create memory problems.

00:00:26.072 --> 00:00:31.852
Spark distributes work across multiple processes and often multiple machines.

00:00:31.852 --> 00:00:38.337
The driver coordinates the job, while executors perform tasks on partitions of the data.

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:44.412
This architecture becomes valuable when the data or workload is too large for one machine or when a platform

00:00:44.412 --> 00:00:47.680
needs scalable parallel processing.
